---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Contact from '../components/Contact.astro';

const sanitizeEmailCode = `function sanitizeEmail(email) {
  if (!email || !email.includes('@')) return '';
  let [user, domain] = email.toString().trim().toLowerCase().split('@');
  const commonFixes = {
    'gmaia.lcom': 'gmail.com', 'gnail.com': 'gmail.com', 'gmai.com': 'gmail.com',
    'gamil.com': 'gmail.com', 'gmal.com': 'gmail.com', 'hotml.com': 'hotmail.com'
  };
  if (commonFixes[domain]) domain = commonFixes[domain];
  return \`\${user}@\${domain}\`;
}`;
---

<Layout
  title="Resilient Infrastructure · Identity Resolution & Chaos-Tolerant Systems · Yasser Gandhi"
  description="Technical proof of concept: Identity Resolution Engine for fragmented multi-platform user data, and Chaos-Tolerant Infrastructure using TypeScript/Zod contracts to prevent production database contamination."
  keywords="Identity Resolution Engine, User Data Deduplication, Email Sanitization, Chaos-Tolerant Architecture, TypeScript Zod Contracts, Data Pipeline, LLM Integration, Production Database Protection, Friction Scan"
>
  <Nav />
  <main>
    <section id="resilient-infrastructure">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-label">Technical Proof of Concept</div>
        <h1 style="font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.5rem); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Resilient Infrastructure</h1>
        <p style="color: var(--gray); font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 4rem; max-width: 600px;">
          Two backend architectures that prevent user data chaos from becoming a production emergency.
        </p>

        <!-- PoC 01: Identity Resolution Engine -->
        <div class="project-card" style="margin-bottom: 4rem;">
          <div class="project-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-production">PoC 01</span>
            <span class="badge badge-live">Backend Architecture</span>
          </div>
          <h2>The Identity Resolution Engine</h2>
          <div class="project-body">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
              Multi-platform SaaS products accumulate a critical technical debt that lives in a
              blind spot: <strong style="color: var(--white-warm);">fragmented user identities.</strong>
              A single real user registers as
              <code style="color: var(--orange-light); font-family: var(--font-mono);">john@gmaia.lcom</code>,
              then as
              <code style="color: var(--orange-light); font-family: var(--font-mono);">john@gnail.com</code>,
              then tries to recover a password with a third variant. Your system treats them as
              three different failed users. Your churn metrics are wrong. Your segmentation is corrupt.
              Your re-engagement campaigns are firing at ghost accounts.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
              The Identity Resolution Engine unifies fragmented, typo-corrupted, or format-broken user
              records into a <strong style="color: var(--white-warm);">Single Source of Truth (SSOT)</strong>
              using advanced heuristic logic — without introducing heavy authentication debt or
              breaking existing integrations. It resolves the chaos at the data ingestion layer
              before it poisons your analytics, your CRM, and your LLM training data.
            </p>
            <p style="color: var(--orange-light); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">
              // Core sanitization function — deployed at the input boundary
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="adr-code-block"><pre set:html={sanitizeEmailCode} /></div>

          <div class="project-body" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
            <p>
              This is not input validation. This is
              <strong style="color: var(--white-warm);">identity resolution at scale.</strong>
              Paired with a deduplication pass on historical records and a conflict-resolution
              strategy for accounts that share normalized emails, this engine transforms your
              user table from a noise floor into a reliable analytical instrument.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="tech-stack" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <span class="tech-tag">JavaScript / TypeScript</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">Heuristic Pattern Matching</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">SSOT Architecture</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">Data Deduplication</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">Input Boundary Design</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- PoC 02: Chaos-Tolerant Infrastructure -->
        <div class="project-card">
          <div class="project-meta">
            <span class="badge badge-production">PoC 02</span>
            <span class="badge badge-live">Backend Architecture</span>
          </div>
          <h2>Chaos-Tolerant Infrastructure</h2>
          <div class="project-body">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
              Real users do not interact with your software predictably. They submit empty forms,
              paste garbled data, trigger edge cases your QA never anticipated, and send malformed
              requests at the exact moment your LLM integration is processing a batch. When your
              infrastructure has no tolerance for this chaos, the result is
              <strong style="color: var(--white-warm);">production database contamination</strong> —
              corrupted records, broken API chains, and downstream failures that take hours to trace.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
              The solution is not more error logging. It is
              <strong style="color: var(--white-warm);">strict data contracts enforced at every system
              boundary.</strong> Using TypeScript type safety and Zod runtime validation schemas,
              we define the exact shape of every data object the system accepts, rejects, or
              transforms. No dirty record touches the production database. No malformed payload
              reaches an LLM API call.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
              Dynamic segmentation by user session sections further isolates erratic behavior:
              a user in an inconsistent state is routed to a recovery flow rather than allowed
              to corrupt shared state. This prevents a single bad actor — or a single confused
              user — from propagating failures across the system graph.
            </p>

            <div class="adr-quote">
              The contract is not a safety net. It is the architecture. When the contract is strict,
              chaos becomes a handled input state, not an incident report.
            </div>

            <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
              This pattern is production-proven in systems that integrate with LLMs under
              real-user load, where the cost of a malformed payload is not just a 400 error —
              it is a corrupted training data record, a wasted API credit, or a broken user
              session that produces measurable churn.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="tech-stack">
            <span class="tech-tag">TypeScript</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">Zod Schema Contracts</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">Dynamic Segmentation</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">LLM API Integration</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">Session Isolation</span>
            <span class="tech-tag">Fault Mitigation</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-top: 4rem; text-align: center;">
          <a href="mailto:yassergandhi.dev@gmail.com?subject=Resilient%20Infrastructure%20Inquiry" class="btn-primary">
            [ Discuss Your Infrastructure Challenge ]
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <hr class="divider" />
    <Contact />
  </main>
</Layout>
